### Inter-Thread Communication: Producer-Consumer Architecture in Java

#### 1. Why `wait()` and `notify()` Reside in `java.lang.Object`:
Unlike `Thread.sleep()`, which operates purely on the current thread, `wait()` and `notify()` operate on **object monitors** (locks). Since every object in Java has a monitor, these methods are defined in the root `Object` class so any shared resource can coordinate thread access.

#### 2. Detailed Method Comparison:
| Method | Monitor Lock Status | State Change | Waking Condition |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`wait()`** | **Releases** the monitor lock immediately. | `RUNNABLE` $\rightarrow$ `WAITING` | Another thread calls `notify()` or `notifyAll()`. |
| **`notify()`** | **Retains** lock until synchronized block finishes. | Wakes **one** waiting thread. | Selected thread moves from `WAITING` to `BLOCKED`. |
| **`notifyAll()`** | **Retains** lock until synchronized block finishes. | Wakes **all** waiting threads. | All threads move to `BLOCKED` and compete for lock. |
| **`sleep(ms)`** | **Retains** lock (does NOT release). | `RUNNABLE` $\rightarrow$ `TIMED_WAITING` | Specified millisecond interval elapses. |

#### 3. Complete Production Implementation: Classic Producer-Consumer Bounded Buffer:
```java
class SharedQueue {
    private int data;
    private boolean hasData = false;

    public synchronized void produce(int value) {
        // Must use 'while' loop instead of 'if' to guard against spurious wakeups
        while (hasData) {
            try {
                wait(); // Wait until consumer consumes existing data
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }

        data = value;
        hasData = true;
        System.out.println("PRODUCED: " + data);
        notify(); // Notify waiting consumer
    }

    public synchronized int consume() {
        while (!hasData) {
            try {
                wait(); // Wait until producer produces data
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }

        hasData = false;
        System.out.println("CONSUMED: " + data);
        notify(); // Notify waiting producer
        return data;
    }
}

public class ProducerConsumerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SharedQueue queue = new SharedQueue();

        Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                queue.produce(i);
            }
        }, "Producer-Thread");

        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                queue.consume();
            }
        }, "Consumer-Thread");

        producer.start();
        consumer.start();
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{wait() and notify() eliminate CPU-wasting spinlocks through lock-releasing notification.}$$
